WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.555
MCP security protects the full integration path between AI applications, users, tools, resources, servers, identities, secrets, and downstream systems.

2
00:00:10.555 --> 00:00:19.047
The security boundary changes because AI-assisted behavior can discover resources, call tools, and influence real systems.

3
00:00:19.047 --> 00:00:30.713
A model answer is no longer just a paragraph; it may be connected to actions and data flows that require application security, identity, operations, and governance controls.

4
00:00:30.713 --> 00:00:36.110
Strong MCP security starts with architecture visibility and trust boundaries.

5
00:00:36.110 --> 00:00:40.157
Teams should map data flow, authority flow, and action flow.

6
00:00:40.157 --> 00:00:51.188
They should identify hosts, clients, servers, tools, resources, prompts, users, service identities, secrets, local files, cloud services, and downstream APIs.

7
00:00:51.188 --> 00:00:58.569
That map gives the organization a shared view of what the agent can read, what it can call, and what it can change.

8
00:00:58.569 --> 00:01:02.458
Prompt wording alone is not a reliable security boundary.

9
00:01:02.458 --> 00:01:17.457
Authorization, least privilege, consent, tool allowlisting, server provenance, argument validation, resource filtering, tenant isolation, approval gates, logging, and monitoring must be enforced outside the model.

10
00:01:17.457 --> 00:01:26.266
Tool descriptions and external content should be treated as security-relevant inputs, not automatically trusted instructions.

11
00:01:26.266 --> 00:01:34.123
Data minimization and secrets handling help prevent sensitive information from entering places it does not belong.

12
00:01:34.123 --> 00:01:36.345
The goal is controlled adoption.

13
00:01:36.345 --> 00:01:44.360
MCP can make AI applications more useful, but useful systems need governance, recoverability, and incident response.

14
00:01:44.360 --> 00:01:56.661
Treat tools like integrations, servers like supply-chain dependencies, resources like governed data, and model-generated actions like requests that require policy enforcement.

15
00:01:56.661 --> 00:02:08.724
Done well, MCP becomes auditable, privacy-aware, and aligned with organizational risk decisions instead of being an uncontrolled bridge between a model and production systems.
